WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.234
Welcome to Building Defender XDR,

2
00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:06.212
a developer workbook in the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

3
00:00:06.212 --> 00:00:10.726
This is Chapter 7: identity
signals and automation boundaries.

4
00:00:10.726 --> 00:00:13.115
The same account seen by the endpoint,

5
00:00:13.115 --> 00:00:16.275
the domain controller and
a cloud app is one story.

6
00:00:16.275 --> 00:00:19.456
What Defender may do about it
on its own is a decision the

7
00:00:19.456 --> 00:00:22.913
authorizing official made, and
the settings have to match it.

8
00:00:22.913 --> 00:00:26.415
We'll start from the guide's
cross-source query and Learn's

9
00:00:26.415 --> 00:00:30.663
automation levels and exclusions,
read both checks, and walk two labs.

10
00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:33.296
The chapter is a draft awaiting review;

11
00:00:33.296 --> 00:00:37.120
the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

12
00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:42.207
Why these two together?

13
00:00:42.207 --> 00:00:45.082
Because they keep detection
and response joined up.

14
00:00:45.082 --> 00:00:50.070
Detection should see one story across
the endpoint, identity and cloud apps.

15
00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:54.382
And response should stay inside the
boundary the authorizing official set.

16
00:00:54.382 --> 00:00:58.806
By the end of this chapter you can show
that identity and cloud-app sources are

17
00:00:58.806 --> 00:01:03.162
feeding incidents, that Defender's
automation matches the response ladder,

18
00:01:03.162 --> 00:01:08.070
and that every exclusion is approved, with
every emergency access account protected.

19
00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:11.051
It's for the developers,
the security operations

20
00:01:11.051 --> 00:01:14.310
lead who owns both gates,
and the platform owner.

21
00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.372
Here's what's already there.

22
00:01:19.372 --> 00:01:23.419
For signals, the guide's query
joins alert evidence to alert info,

23
00:01:23.419 --> 00:01:27.923
and keeps the users with alerts from
more than one service in seven days.

24
00:01:27.923 --> 00:01:31.976
In GCC, Learn lists App Governance's
alerts as roadmap items,

25
00:01:31.976 --> 00:01:36.360
and Cloud Apps behaviors aren't
available in the behavior table.

26
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:40.048
For automation, each device
group has one of five levels,

27
00:01:40.048 --> 00:01:45.098
from full remediation to no automated
response; Microsoft recommends full.

28
00:01:45.098 --> 00:01:49.021
User exclusions are for service
and emergency admin accounts,

29
00:01:49.021 --> 00:01:51.209
and exclusions reduce protection.

30
00:01:51.209 --> 00:01:55.872
Contain User needs the
sensor at 10.8470 or later.

31
00:01:55.872 --> 00:01:59.716
Attack disruption's GCC
availability isn't specified,

32
00:01:59.716 --> 00:02:04.940
and the portal holds these settings;
Learn names no Graph API for them.

33
00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:09.690
What's left to you?

34
00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:14.571
The query returns users in no order, and
can't tell you a source has gone quiet,

35
00:02:14.571 --> 00:02:16.618
so the kit sorts a priority list,

36
00:02:16.618 --> 00:02:20.240
and fails when no row names
a source beyond the endpoint.

37
00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.715
The portal shows five levels
but doesn't know the ladder,

38
00:02:23.715 --> 00:02:26.586
so the kit's ladder says
workstations get full,

39
00:02:26.586 --> 00:02:30.515
the pilot gets semi for all folders,
and servers get a semi level.

40
00:02:30.515 --> 00:02:34.931
Exclusions sit in the portal
unreviewed, so each needs an approver

41
00:02:34.931 --> 00:02:38.865
and a review date, and every
emergency account must be there.

42
00:02:38.865 --> 00:02:42.421
And Contain User silently
doesn't run on an old sensor,

43
00:02:42.421 --> 00:02:45.130
so each device's version is checked.

44
00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:49.843
Two checks.

45
00:02:49.843 --> 00:02:54.654
The signals check runs the guide's
cross-source query and writes a priority list.

46
00:02:54.654 --> 00:02:58.804
An emergency access account goes
first, with one instruction:

47
00:02:58.804 --> 00:03:03.442
page the incident response lead,
and a human decides any containment.

48
00:03:03.442 --> 00:03:06.068
Three or more sources is a priority.

49
00:03:06.068 --> 00:03:09.730
The automation check reads
three files and calls nothing:

50
00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:14.918
the device groups export, the attack
disruption exclusions, and the device states.

51
00:03:14.918 --> 00:03:19.342
A workstation group below full passes
only with a recorded exception.

52
00:03:19.342 --> 00:03:24.340
The exports come from two portal pages,
copied into the template columns.

53
00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:29.140
Who's involved?

54
00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:32.647
The authorizing official
decides attack disruption as a

55
00:03:32.647 --> 00:03:36.153
class on the response ladder,
and approves each exclusion.

56
00:03:36.153 --> 00:03:39.148
The security operations
lead owns both gates:

57
00:03:39.148 --> 00:03:42.853
the weekly hunt, and a quarterly
review of the exclusions.

58
00:03:42.853 --> 00:03:47.440
The platform owner sets the device group
levels with the security operations lead.

59
00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.618
One note: the ladder's mapping
is the program's design,

60
00:03:50.618 --> 00:03:55.052
and the authorizing official's attack
disruption decision comes first.

61
00:03:55.052 --> 00:03:59.130
The kit checks the settings against
it; it doesn't make the decision.

62
00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:04.980
Lab 1, in the kit's folder.

63
00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.355
Step 1 runs the signals check.

64
00:04:07.355 --> 00:04:10.294
The first row is an
emergency access account,

65
00:04:10.294 --> 00:04:13.630
with identity and cloud-app
alerts in the same week.

66
00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:17.843
Nobody's playbook touches it; the
incident response lead decides.

67
00:04:17.843 --> 00:04:22.542
Then a user seen by three sources,
a priority, and one seen by two.

68
00:04:22.542 --> 00:04:25.109
The last line says identity or cloud-app

69
00:04:25.109 --> 00:04:28.500
sources are feeding alerts,
so the check passes.

70
00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:35.875
Step 2 runs the automation
check, and finds four things.

71
00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:41.375
The ring 3 workstation group sits at a semi
level with no exception: off the ladder.

72
00:04:41.375 --> 00:04:45.612
The scanner subnet's exclusion
passed its review date in October.

73
00:04:45.612 --> 00:04:49.962
The second emergency access account
isn't in the identity exclusions,

74
00:04:49.962 --> 00:04:52.362
so attack disruption could contain it.

75
00:04:52.362 --> 00:04:58.713
And one device runs a sensor below
10.8470, so Contain User won't act there.

76
00:04:58.713 --> 00:05:03.772
Step 3 reads the ladder the groups are
checked against: full for workstations,

77
00:05:03.772 --> 00:05:08.330
semi for all folders for the pilot,
and any semi level for servers.

78
00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:15.012
Lab 2 uses a test
tenant, never production,

79
00:05:15.012 --> 00:05:19.755
with Defender for Identity on at
least one test domain controller.

80
00:05:19.755 --> 00:05:22.055
Sign in and run the signals check.

81
00:05:22.055 --> 00:05:26.506
In a quiet test tenant the query may
return no rows, and the check fails:

82
00:05:26.506 --> 00:05:31.114
that says no identity or cloud-app
alert met an endpoint alert this week,

83
00:05:31.114 --> 00:05:36.268
not that the sensors are broken; check
sensor health in the Identities settings.

84
00:05:36.268 --> 00:05:40.674
Then copy the two portal pages into the
template columns: the device groups,

85
00:05:40.674 --> 00:05:45.293
and the automated response exclusions
with their approvers and review dates.

86
00:05:45.293 --> 00:05:46.893
Run the automation check.

87
00:05:46.893 --> 00:05:49.580
Nothing changed, so you just sign out.

88
00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:56.692
Here's what these gates prove,
against NIST's catalog.

89
00:05:56.692 --> 00:06:00.411
Identity and cloud-app alerts
correlating with the endpoint's

90
00:06:00.411 --> 00:06:04.343
supports system monitoring and
correlating audit repositories.

91
00:06:04.343 --> 00:06:08.842
Emergency access accounts flagged for
a person supports account monitoring.

92
00:06:08.842 --> 00:06:12.058
Each device group's level
matching the ladder supports

93
00:06:12.058 --> 00:06:15.204
automated incident handling
and automated response.

94
00:06:15.204 --> 00:06:20.163
And exclusions approved and reviewed,
with every emergency account excluded,

95
00:06:20.163 --> 00:06:23.443
supports automated incident
handling done safely.

96
00:06:23.443 --> 00:06:26.631
The indicators are FedRAMP's,
as the guide maps them.

97
00:06:26.631 --> 00:06:30.800
The records support these controls;
the assessor decides them.

98
00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:35.950
What breaks in production?

99
00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:40.011
Attack disruption containing an
emergency access account locks you

100
00:06:40.011 --> 00:06:43.862
out during the incident; every
emergency account must be excluded.

101
00:06:43.862 --> 00:06:48.887
A server group at full means a server
remediated or isolated without a person;

102
00:06:48.887 --> 00:06:51.713
the ladder allows
servers only semi levels.

103
00:06:51.713 --> 00:06:55.815
An exclusion nobody reviews
quietly withdraws protection;

104
00:06:55.815 --> 00:06:57.949
a review date ahead is required.

105
00:06:57.949 --> 00:07:00.812
An old sensor means
Contain User does nothing.

106
00:07:00.812 --> 00:07:05.467
And when the identity sensors stop,
the query goes quiet and looks calm,

107
00:07:05.467 --> 00:07:08.810
so no source beyond the
endpoint is a failure.

108
00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:13.910
Here's what done looks like.

109
00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:18.810
The cross-source query names at least one
identity or cloud-app source each week.

110
00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:23.398
Every emergency access account in the
settings is an identity exclusion.

111
00:07:23.398 --> 00:07:27.197
Every device group is at the
ladder's level, or has an exception.

112
00:07:27.197 --> 00:07:30.785
Every exclusion has an approver
and a review date ahead.

113
00:07:30.785 --> 00:07:34.935
And every device runs a sensor at
or above the Contain User minimum.

114
00:07:34.935 --> 00:07:38.066
Next, Chapter 8 runs every
check in one command,

115
00:07:38.066 --> 00:07:43.010
computes the guide's metrics, and
records it all for the security officer.

116
00:07:43.010 --> 00:07:44.170
Thanks for watching.
